WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.840
Local language-model performance is a user experience, not one benchmark number.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:08.160
Time to first token measures the delay before useful output begins.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.725
That delay includes model loading, queueing, tokenization, and prompt processing.

00:00:12.725 --> 00:00:14.975
Generation speed, commonly reported as tokens per second,

00:00:14.975 --> 00:00:18.178
describes how quickly output continues after generation starts.

00:00:18.178 --> 00:00:22.510
A system can generate quickly yet feel slow if it spends a long time ingesting a large

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:23.080
prompt.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.550
Throughput is the amount of useful work completed over time,

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.400
especially when several requests or documents are processed.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.555
Latency is the delay experienced by an individual request.

00:00:31.555 --> 00:00:35.580
Batching and concurrency may improve throughput while making one user wait longer.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.779
Stability also belongs in the performance definition.

00:00:37.779 --> 00:00:41.290
A fast configuration that runs out of memory, overheats, crashes,

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:44.610
or produces unacceptable output is not an optimized system.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.425
Synthetic benchmarks are useful for controlled comparison,

00:00:47.425 --> 00:00:51.150
but real workflows include context length, retrieval results, code files,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.655
document parsing, network interfaces, and repeated sessions.

00:00:54.655 --> 00:00:58.400
Measure the task that matters: interactive chat, coding completion, summarization,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.948
embeddings, or batch extraction.

00:00:59.948 --> 00:01:04.045
Record model, quantization, runtime, prompt, context setting, hardware state,

00:01:04.045 --> 00:01:06.745
and temperature so a result can be reproduced.

00:01:06.745 --> 00:01:08.415
Optimization starts with a baseline.

00:01:08.415 --> 00:01:10.520
Run a repeatable workload, observe each stage,

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.230
and identify the constrained resource before changing anything.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:17.715
Then alter one variable and compare speed, memory, quality, power, and stability.

00:01:17.715 --> 00:01:20.720
Guessing encourages expensive upgrades and random settings;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.950
measurement turns tuning into an engineering process.
